In Nevada, a landlord has **30 days** to return the security deposit. Within that time they must either return the deposit or give the tenant a written, itemized list of what they're keeping. The 30 days start once the lease ends and the tenant has moved out.

**Other Nevada rules that may help:**
- **Penalty for missing the deadline:** If the landlord doesn't return the deposit on time, a court can make them pay the whole deposit back, plus extra damages of up to the deposit amount again. That's up to 2x the deposit in total.
- **Limit on deposits:** Everything held as security together can't be more than 3 months' rent. That includes any prepaid last month's rent or surety bond.
- **Move-in record:** Every written lease must provide for a signed record of what's in the unit and what condition it's in. Photos aren't required.

**Source:** PropInspect's Nevada guide, https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/nevada, last reviewed October 12, 2026. It's based on Nevada Revised Statutes § 118A.242 ([official text](https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-118A.html#NRS118ASec242)).

